<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  App Transport Security exemption for the webview, and nothing else.

	  A self-hosted la musica server is plain http on an address only the user
	  knows: a LAN IP, a Tailscale/MagicDNS name, or 127.0.0.1:8000 for the
	  desktop shell's own backend. TLS is not optional for the project to run
	  without it - the server ships without a certificate, so every request the
	  React UI makes (fetch, WebSocket, audio/video playback) is http:// or
	  ws://. ATS blocks exactly those requests by default, which is what makes
	  the app unusable against a real install.

	  NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent keeps the exemption inside the webview:
	  the shell's native URLSession calls (there are none today - lib.rs has no
	  HTTP client) stay under full ATS, and NSExceptionDomains cannot be used
	  here because the host is chosen by the user at runtime, not by us.

	  Tauri merges this file into the generated plist for both iOS
	  (`tauri ios build`, which is told about it explicitly through
	  `bundle.iOS.infoPlist` in tauri.conf.json) and the macOS .app bundle,
	  so one file covers both Apple platforms. Android is unaffected and
	  documented in desktop/README.md.
	-->
	<!--
	  Background execution, which an app is otherwise denied the moment the user
	  switches away or locks the phone — and a suspended app serves nothing: the
	  embedded backend stops answering until the app is resumed.

	  `audio` is the mode this app can claim honestly, because it IS a music
	  player: while audio plays, the process keeps running, which is what keeps
	  the local backend (and Soulseek sharing, and an in-flight import) alive.
	  The shell's "keep hosting in the background" setting activates a playback
	  audio session even with nothing playing — the silent-audio trick, marked in
	  desktop/src-tauri/src/mobile_backend.rs as a sideload-only affordance,
	  off by default, and not something an App Store build may ship turned on.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>audio</string>
	</array>

	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>

	<!--
	  The name under the home-screen icon. Without it the generated plist has
	  only CFBundleName, and iOS falls back to that - which today happens to be
	  "la musica" because the CLI maps PRODUCT_NAME from productName by
	  template. Both keys are named here so the sideloaded IPA shows the
	  product's own name rather than whatever the template does with it.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>la musica</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>la musica</string>

	<!--
	  Export-compliance declaration for the App Store question every upload
	  asks. `false` is the honest answer for this app: it implements no
	  cryptography of its own - HTTPS, the keychain and the TLS stack are the
	  system's, and a self-hosted server is reached over plain http. Apple
	  accepts this key in place of answering the question per build, which
	  matters for the sideload flow (an ad-hoc re-sign of our unsigned IPA)
	  because a re-signed bundle cannot be edited to add it afterwards without
	  breaking the signature.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	  Orientation, iPhone and iPad separately because iOS reads the `~ipad`
	  suffix. iPhone omits upside-down, the usual choice: nothing in the UI is
	  drawn for it (the shell rotates fine, the player bar and library just
	  ride along) and iOS only rotates to upside-down when an app asks. iPad
	  gets all four - the library and the tag editors have the room for either
	  landscape. Declared here instead of left to tauri-cli's project.yml
	  template so a reader of this repo knows what the app ships with.
	-->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
